---
/**
 * foodcraft / HeroBO — "Sipsip Tea Bar"'s arrival.
 *
 * ROLE. The page's opening statement and its only h1. The whole hero sits
 * INSIDE one ultraviolet slab: a 36px-radius panel filled with --accent,
 * holding two columns — on the left a blush PILL eyebrow, a Baloo 2 headline
 * whose closing phrase turns BLUSH (a colour change, never an italic), a lede
 * and two actions; on the right one PERFECT CIRCLE photograph. Lifted verbatim
 * from the element carrying data-component="HERO" in
 * "Sipsip Tea Bar Landing.dc.html": section padding 20px 28px 0, panel
 * background --accent at border-radius 36px with padding 56px 52px, grid
 * minmax(0,1.05fr) minmax(0,.95fr) at gap 44px with align-items:center and
 * overflow:hidden, h1 clamp(40px,5.4vw,76px)/1.04 with margin .2em 0 .26em,
 * lede 17.5px/1.72 at 42ch with 30px beneath it, actions 11px apart, and a
 * right column at aspect-ratio 1/1 and border-radius 50%.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every path below is declared in components/editor/
 * genericContentSchema.ts group id 'hero', so each is live in the sidebar AND
 * inline):
 *   hero.image              — data-image-field, the circular photograph
 *   hero.kicker             — the eyebrow, drawn as the blush pill
 *   hero.headline           — the h1, set:html so an owner em takes the blush
 *   hero.headlineLines.${i} — the SAME h1, when hero.headline holds nothing but
 *                             the derived business name; see THE HEADLINE below
 *   hero.sub                — the lede
 *   hero.cta1.text + data-href-field hero.cta1.href — the cream filled button
 *   hero.cta2.text + data-href-field hero.cta2.href — the outlined button
 *
 * DERIVED, never stored and never editable:
 *   · the photograph's accessible name — layout.businessName and nothing else
 *   · both button hrefs, resolved through onPage() against the ids PageBO
 *     actually renders
 *   · which of the two headline paths the h1 binds to
 *   · the blush tail, when the owner has not marked one (see THE SIGNATURE)
 *   · the flat variant (no photograph ⇒ one column), below
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   · No copy of any kind is written by this file. The design's demo strings
 *     ("Shaken fresh, sealed to go.", "See the drinks", "Contact us") are the
 *     DESIGNER filling in their own template. A real tea bar gets an arrival
 *     that says only what that tea bar said, so every guard sits OUTSIDE its
 *     tag: no pill over nothing, no empty action row, and no purple slab with
 *     a hole in it where a photograph nobody uploaded would have gone.
 *   · NO META / PILL ROW under the actions. hero.meta1, hero.meta2 and
 *     hero.trustLine are all declared by the schema, but THIS design draws no
 *     such row here — its four facts live in the TRUST section below. Inventing
 *     a row to use up a declared field would be a layout the designer did not
 *     draw. The fields stay editable in the sidebar and simply have no element
 *     here.
 *   · NO STAR ROW of any kind. The design has none here, and this section has
 *     no numeric rating field to draw one from; a star that is not counted out
 *     of a rating is a fabricated claim. The schema's placeholder on hero.meta1
 *     is literally '★★★★★ rated on Google' — a placeholder is not a default,
 *     lib/derive-content-defaults.ts emits no meta1 for exactly that reason,
 *     and this file prints no star glyph at all.
 *   · hero.tag / hero.photos.* — not declared by the schema. A data-field on a
 *     path no sidebar input owns looks editable and focuses nothing. (hero.photos
 *     is read as a display FALLBACK for the image source only; the HOOK is
 *     always hero.image, the path the sidebar's picker writes.)
 *   · .reveal. This section is above the fold; it is painted, not animated in.
 *   · An aria-label on the section. Naming it would make it a landmark
 *     announced with a word I wrote ("Hero"); the h1 already titles the region.
 *   · target="_blank". The design's demo markup carries it because its primary
 *     is hard-wired to a Messenger URL; here the href is the owner's and may be
 *     an in-page anchor, so navigation is left alone (the family idiom).
 *   · A .band-* class. The wrapper's three bands (.band-pink .band-sage
 *     .band-lilac) each carry their own text colours and are the right tool for
 *     a section that stands on one of the answering grounds — but this hero
 *     stands on --accent, which has no band, and it is a PANEL inside the paper
 *     ground rather than a full-bleed band. So the panel declares
 *     background-color:var(--accent) with color:var(--on-accent) as its own
 *     pairing, which is the same on-colour discipline the bands use, applied to
 *     the one ground the wrapper does not pre-declare.
 *
 * THE THREE HUES, as this section uses them (no literal hex anywhere in this
 * file — R1):
 *   --accent  is the panel. Everything below sits ON it.
 *   --pink    is a GROUND twice over — the eyebrow pill and the headline tail —
 *             and is TEXT exactly once, on the h1, where it is large type. It
 *             is never body text here.
 *   --sage    is not used. The design does not put it in the hero, and this
 *             file does not invent a place for it.
 *
 * COLOUR / CONTRAST — COMPUTED, not estimated (WCAG 2.x sRGB relative
 * luminance, over the hexes PageBO declares):
 *   --on-cream on the cream primary          10.81:1
 *   --plum on the --pink eyebrow pill         7.41:1
 *   --on-accent on --accent (h1, ghost label)  5.63:1  — agrees with PageBO
 *   --lilac lede on --accent                   4.77:1
 *   ghost label on its .08 paper hover wash    4.77:1  (the wash resolves to
 *     sRGB 134,92,174 over --accent; anything heavier than ~.10 drops the
 *     label under 4.5, which is why the wash is this light)
 *   --pink headline tail on --accent           3.93:1  — this is the SAME pair
 *     PageBO flags as failing, and it fails the 4.5 BODY floor exactly as the
 *     wrapper says. It is used here only inside the h1, whose smallest size in
 *     any query is 28px at weight 700 — far above the 18.66px-bold threshold
 *     for large text, where the floor is 3:1. Nothing else in this file puts
 *     the pink on the purple, and nothing here puts the purple on the pink.
 *
 * ONE NON-TEXT PAIR TO REVIEW. The ghost button's edge is the design's own
 * `2px solid rgba(paper, .55)`, which resolves to sRGB 196,171,209 over the
 * panel and measures 2.86:1 against it — just under the 3:1 that WCAG 1.4.11
 * asks of a UI component boundary. The design is the specification, so the
 * value is reproduced rather than quietly nudged; the control is still
 * identifiable by its label, which is --on-accent at 5.63:1. Raising the alpha
 * to .60 gives 3.12:1 and is a one-value change if a reviewer wants the bar
 * cleared.
 *
 * THE EYEBROW USES --plum, NOT --on-pink, and that is the design and not a
 * slip. The design's hero pill fills with the blush and labels it in the value
 * PageBO names --plum; its pink BUTTON further down the page fills with the
 * same blush and labels it in --on-pink instead. Both clear AA (7.41 and
 * 9.96), the design distinguishes them, and this file follows the design.
 *
 * THE HEADLINE, and why one heading reads two paths. This design's headline is
 * ONE flowing sentence whose closing phrase turns blush — not a stack of lines
 * — so the h1 is hero.headline rendered through set:html and the emphasis is an
 * em the OWNER types.
 * The catch is WHICH FIELD actually holds the owner's headline, because two
 * layers upstream disagree:
 *   · lib/derive-content-defaults.ts auto-fills hero.headline with the BARE
 *     BUSINESS NAME on every named site (`headline: name || undefined`), and
 *     lib/astro-builder.ts's mergeShallow lets that derived value stand
 *     wherever the owner left the field unset.
 *   · lib/services/groq.service.ts — the extraction that writes the copy for a
 *     real submission — emits hero.headlineLines and NEVER hero.headline.
 * So "always prefer hero.headline" prints the business name and drops the
 * authored headline on the floor for exactly the sites that have one. The rule
 * is therefore: hero.headline wins whenever it is AUTHORED, meaning anything
 * other than the business name the derive layer fills in. When it is that
 * auto-fill (or empty), the surviving headlineLines rows win — flowed INLINE
 * inside the same h1, because this design's headline is a sentence and not a
 * stack — each carrying its ORIGINAL array index, so dropping a blank row 2
 * cannot misdirect an edit into row 3 (R6). The decorative part — which row
 * gets the blush — uses the DISPLAY position instead, because "the last one you
 * can see" is what the eye is being told.
 * The field a click opens is always the field the visible text came from:
 * binding hero.headlineLines.0 to text that arrived as hero.headline (or the
 * reverse) aims an inline edit at a slot the sidebar is not showing.
 *
 * THE SIGNATURE, MADE REACHABLE. The design's h1 is two-tone — cream, then a
 * blush close ("sealed to go.") — and it got there because the DESIGNER typed a
 * span into their own markup. No owner is going to type a tag into a text box,
 * so without a fallback the headline renders one flat colour on every real site
 * and one of the two things that makes this template recognisable never ships.
 * When the owner has NOT marked emphasis, the tail is derived from THEIR OWN
 * sentence and adds not one word — it only decides where the sentence turns.
 * An explicit em always wins; this fills a silence. Note the design's own
 * headline turns at its comma, which is exactly what the derivation below does
 * with it.
 * IT IS A COLOUR CHANGE AND NOT AN ITALIC. Baloo 2 ships no italic, so asking
 * for one synthesises an oblique that fights its rounded terminals; every
 * emphasis rule below therefore states font-style:normal explicitly, because
 * an em is italic by user-agent default.
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5). The arrival renders when it has something to SAY or SHOW:
 * a headline, a lede, or the photograph. It deliberately does NOT count the
 * eyebrow (derive-content-defaults builds one from city + trade for sites with
 * neither name, tagline nor upload) and does NOT count the two buttons (derive
 * sets cta1/cta2 text UNCONDITIONALLY, so counting either makes the gate a
 * no-op that always passes).
 * FOR REVIEW — this is the one section in the family whose gate counts the
 * headline, against the letter of R5. Here the headline IS the body: it is the
 * document's only h1, and a hero dropped for having "only" an h1 takes the
 * page's heading with it and opens the site on the trust row. A purple panel
 * carrying a real headline is not an empty section with a heading over it.
 *
 * NO PHOTOGRAPH ⇒ ONE COLUMN. The circle is a COLUMN here, not a background. If
 * the owner uploaded nothing, rendering it anyway leaves a ~470px purple disc
 * on a purple panel — a visible hole. So the plate is dropped entirely and the
 * grid collapses to a single track, with the copy held at 523px: that is the
 * width the left column has IN THE DESIGN, at the design's own measure
 * ((1200 − 56 gutter − 104 panel padding − 44 gap) × 1.05/2.00), so the
 * sentence is not re-flowed to a width the design never gave it.
 * Both that 523px and the ~470px disc above are the DESIGN's box, not this
 * build's. PageBO's .wrap is `width: min(var(--maxw), 92vw)` with NO padding,
 * so at the full 1200px measure the live two-column left track is ~552px and
 * the circle ~500px; the 28px gutter the design writes into its own max-width
 * is 4vw here and only bites below 1304px. The design's 523px cap is kept
 * deliberately rather than widened to match — see the LADDER note below, whose
 * figures are measured off `92vw` and are this build's, not the design's.
 *
 * THE SECTION HAS NO BOTTOM PADDING, and that is the design, not an oversight.
 * The HERO element ends at `padding:20px 28px 0`; the TRUST row below carries
 * its own 22px of top padding, and every other section brings its own top
 * padding from the wrapper's `section` rule. A floor here would double it.
 *
 * TWO VALUES THIS FILE DOES NOT RESTATE, so a reader does not read them as
 * misses. (1) The h1's letter-spacing: the design declares none, and PageBO's
 * heading primitive sets -.01em on every h1–h4 in the template on purpose;
 * that family decision is left to own it rather than being unpicked in one
 * section for -0.76px at 76px. (2) The eyebrow's line-height: it keeps the
 * shared .kick's 1.1, which makes the pill ~3px shorter than the design's
 * browser-default leading (11px x Nunito Sans's ~1.36 normal leading against
 * 11px x 1.1). Only the pill's ground, label, tracking, padding and radius are
 * stated here.
 *
 * CLASS-NAME LAW (not inferable, do not "tidy"): the section is .sip-hero, NOT
 * .hero — lib/genericThemeOverrides.ts force-paints `.hero .btn-ghost` and
 * `.hero .btn-primary:hover` with !important and would strand a label here.
 * Buttons are .btn + .btn--cream / .btn--line (double dash) for the same
 * reason. Never .btn-primary / .btn-ghost / .btn-yellow / .btn-light /
 * .nav-cta / .nav-right / .trust / .testi / .cta-band in this family. The `sip-`
 * prefix is this template's ("Sipsip", and the design's own id on the hero
 * image slot) and is shared with no sibling: BL uses `kc-`, BM uses `tc-`, BN
 * uses `pd-`. An audit had to rename a whole set of classes that were copied
 * verbatim from the template before it.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;

// Anchor guard, copied from the BM sibling and re-pointed at THIS page.
// lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes '#visit' for hero.cta1 and
// '#services' for hero.cta2; this page renders `visit` but not `services`, so
// one is kept and the other is remapped. Resolve every in-page href against
// what PageBO actually draws: keep it when the id exists, remap it when this
// design carries the same block under another id, otherwise fall back to the
// target the design was built around. Off-page hrefs (tel:, mailto:, http)
// pass through untouched.
// Keep PAGE_ANCHORS and ANCHOR_ALIAS in step with PageBO — and with the copies
// in HeaderBO, CtaBandBO and FooterBO. There is no shared import by design (a
// section component in this repo receives only `content` + `layout`); the
// copies must stay identical by hand.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['menu', 'about', 'gallery', 'visit', 'faq', 'main']);
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  location: 'visit', contact: 'visit',
  services: 'menu', rooms: 'menu',
  host: 'about', why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
  book: 'main',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[href.slice(1)];
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(href.slice(1))) return href;
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

const hero = content.hero || {};

// Display fallbacks only — the HOOK below is always hero.image, the path the
// sidebar's image picker writes. The quote escape keeps a filename with an
// apostrophe from closing the url('') early and killing the declaration.
const heroImage = String(hero.image || (hero.photos && hero.photos[0]) || (content.photos && content.photos[0]) || '');
const heroImageCss = heroImage.replace(/'/g, '%27');

const kicker = hero.kicker || '';

// THE HEADLINE — full reasoning in the doc comment. hero.headline is this
// design's field; the rows take over only when hero.headline is nothing but the
// business name derive-content-defaults auto-filled (or is empty), which is the
// shape every AI-written submission arrives in.
const bizName = String(layout?.businessName ?? '').trim();
const headline = String(hero.headline ?? '').trim();
const lineRows: Array<{ text: string; i: number }> = (Array.isArray(hero.headlineLines) ? hero.headlineLines : [])
  .map((t: any, i: number) => ({ text: String(t ?? ''), i }))
  .filter((l: { text: string }) => l.text.trim() !== '');
// …and only when they SAY something hero.headline does not. On the ordinary
// named site both hold the same one line, and the single-line hook keeps it.
const useRows =
  lineRows.length > 0 &&
  (headline === '' || headline === bizName) &&
  lineRows.map((l) => l.text.trim()).join(' ') !== headline;
const hasHeadline = useRows || headline !== '';

// ── THE SIGNATURE, MADE REACHABLE ────────────────────────────────────────
// When the owner has NOT marked emphasis themselves, the tail is derived from
// their OWN sentence and adds not one word:
//   • more than one line                  -> the last line
//   • a comma, dash, colon or semicolon   -> everything after the LAST one
//   • anything else                       -> nothing at all
// That last case is deliberate. A single clause is a single clause, and
// splitting inside one would lean on a word the owner never leaned on — the
// design's own headline turns at "fresh, / sealed to go" precisely because that
// is where its sentence turns, and only the writer knows that point.
const hasOwnEm = (v: string): boolean => /<em[\s>]/i.test(v);
const emphasise = (raw: string): string => {
  if (!raw || hasOwnEm(raw)) return raw;
  const lines = raw.split(/\r?\n/).map((l) => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);
  if (lines.length > 1) {
    const tail = lines.pop() as string;
    return lines.join(' ') + ' <em>' + tail + '</em>';
  }
  // The LAST break wins, so a sentence with two commas emphasises its close
  // rather than its middle. The lookahead keeps a trailing comma from matching.
  const re = /[,;:—–]\s+(?=\S)/g;
  let cut = -1;
  let m: RegExpExecArray | null;
  while ((m = re.exec(raw)) !== null) cut = m.index + m[0].length;
  if (cut <= 0 || cut >= raw.length) return raw;
  return raw.slice(0, cut) + '<em>' + raw.slice(cut) + '</em>';
};
const headlineHtml = emphasise(headline);
// The rows path marks its LAST VISIBLE row instead, and only when no row is
// already carrying the owner's own em.
const rowsOwnEm = lineRows.some((l) => hasOwnEm(l.text));

const sub = hero.sub || '';
const cta1Text = hero.cta1?.text || '';
// The design's primary is its contact action and its secondary points at the
// drinks; those are the two fallbacks when an owner's href resolves to nothing.
const cta1Href = onPage(hero.cta1?.href, '#visit');
const cta2Text = hero.cta2?.text || '';
const cta2Href = onPage(hero.cta2?.href, '#menu');

// The photograph's accessible name is the owner's own words or nothing. With no
// business name there is no honest name for the picture, so it leaves the
// accessibility tree instead of announcing a phrase I invented. Built as ONE
// spread object so a conditional attribute can never end up beside a literal
// (R9 — two components in an earlier family shipped exactly that duplication,
// and the literal aria-hidden won).
const photoA11y: Record<string, string> = bizName
  ? { role: 'img', 'aria-label': bizName }
  : { 'aria-hidden': 'true' };

const hasArrival = !!(hasHeadline || sub || heroImage);
---
{hasArrival && (
<section class={heroImage ? 'sip-hero' : 'sip-hero sip-hero--flat'}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sip-hero-panel">
      <div class="sip-hero-copy">
        {kicker && <span class="kick sip-hero-kick" data-field="hero.kicker">{kicker}</span>}
        {!useRows && headline !== '' && <h1 class="sip-hero-h1" data-field="hero.headline" set:html={headlineHtml}></h1>}
        {useRows && (
          <h1 class="sip-hero-h1">
            {lineRows.map((l, k) => (
              <span class:list={['sip-hero-ln', !rowsOwnEm && k === lineRows.length - 1 && 'sip-hero-ln--em']}><span data-field={`hero.headlineLines.${l.i}`} set:html={l.text}></span>{k === lineRows.length - 1 ? '' : ' '}</span>
            ))}
          </h1>
        )}
        {sub && <p class="lede sip-hero-lede" data-field="hero.sub">{sub}</p>}
        {(cta1Text || cta2Text) && (
          <div class="sip-hero-ctas">
            {cta1Text && (
              <a class="btn btn--cream" href={cta1Href} data-field="hero.cta1.text" data-href-field="hero.cta1.href">{cta1Text}</a>
            )}
            {cta2Text && (
              <a class="btn btn--line sip-hero-ghost" href={cta2Href} data-field="hero.cta2.text" data-href-field="hero.cta2.href">{cta2Text}</a>
            )}
          </div>
        )}
      </div>

      {/* The circle. No upload ⇒ no column at all, rather than a purple disc on
          a purple panel standing in for a picture nobody supplied. */}
      {heroImage && (
        <div class="plate sip-hero-shot">
          <div
            class="ph sip-hero-ph"
            data-image-field="hero.image"
            style={`background-image:url('${heroImageCss}');`}
            {...photoA11y}
          ></div>
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
)}
<style>
  /* Colour, type and metrics come only from PageBO's token vocabulary
     (--paper --accent --on-accent --pink --plum --lilac --disp --r-pill --r-lg
     --paper-rgb) and from its shared primitives (.wrap .kick .lede .btn
     .btn--cream .btn--line .plate .ph). There is no literal hex in this file.
     The two translucent values are written against --paper-rgb, which the theme
     engine owns and repaints in step with --paper, so they track the panel's
     own on-colour instead of drifting away from it.

     HOW THESE RULES WIN, and the one config that would break them. Astro scopes
     a style block with the project's `scopedStyleStrategy`, and this project
     takes the default, 'attribute' — so `.sip-hero-shot` ships as
     `.sip-hero-shot[data-astro-cid-…]`, specificity (0,2,0). The wrapper's
     primitives are `html[data-page="BO"] :where(.plate)`, specificity (0,1,1),
     because :where() contributes nothing; its `section` rule is (0,1,2). Two
     classes beat one, which is how the panel's padding replaces the shared
     section padding, the eyebrow's blush ground replaces .kick's purple ink,
     the lede keeps its 42ch measure against the wrapper's 760 override, and the
     ghost button keeps a cream edge instead of .btn--line's purple one — none
     of it with !important.
     Setting scopedStyleStrategy:'where' in astro.config.mjs would collapse
     every one of those to (0,1,0) and silently hand the wrapper the win. Two-
     class rules (`.sip-hero-ctas .btn`) survive that; the single-class ones do
     not. */

  /* Design: max-width:1200px; margin:0 auto; padding:20px 28px 0. The measure
     and the gutter are .wrap's job; the 20px top and the ZERO floor are this
     section's. See the doc comment on why there is no bottom padding. */
  .sip-hero { padding: 20px 0 0; }

  /* Design: background --accent; border-radius:36px; padding:56px 52px;
     display:grid; grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1.05fr) minmax(0,.95fr);
     gap:44px; align-items:center; overflow:hidden.
     36px is a length, not a token: the design's hero corner is softer than
     --r-lg (26px), which is the card radius everywhere else on this page. The
     ground/ink pair is declared together here for the same reason the wrapper's
     bands declare theirs — so nothing inside has to guess what is legible. */
  .sip-hero-panel {
    background-color: var(--accent);
    color: var(--on-accent);
    border-radius: 36px;
    padding: 56px 52px;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.05fr) minmax(0, .95fr);
    gap: 44px;
    align-items: center;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  /* No photograph ⇒ one track, and the copy holds the measure the left column
     actually has at the design's own width. Letting the sentence run the full
     panel would be a headline this design never draws. */
  .sip-hero--flat .sip-hero-panel { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .sip-hero--flat .sip-hero-copy { max-width: 523px; }

  /* min-width:0 lets a long word wrap inside the track instead of widening it
     and pushing the page sideways. */
  .sip-hero-copy { min-width: 0; }

  /* Design: background --pink; color --plum; font-size:11px; font-weight:700;
     letter-spacing:.14em; text-transform:uppercase; padding:8px 16px;
     border-radius:100px. The face, weight, size, caps and inline-block come
     from the shared .kick and are not restated; what IS restated is everything
     that turns an eyebrow into a pill — plus the label colour, because .kick's
     default is --accent and purple on the blush is 3.93:1, the one pairing this
     palette must never make. max-width keeps a long derived kicker wrapping
     inside the column instead of stretching it. */
  .sip-hero-kick {
    background-color: var(--pink);
    color: var(--plum);
    letter-spacing: .14em;
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border-radius: var(--r-pill);
    max-width: 100%;
  }

  /* Design: font-family Baloo 2; font-weight:700; font-size:clamp(40px,5.4vw,
     76px); line-height:1.04; margin:.2em 0 .26em; color --on-accent. Larger and
     tighter than the shared .h2, which is why this is an h1 rule of its own and
     not that primitive. The design sets NO max-width on the h1 — its column is
     the measure — so none is invented here; the narrow queries below add one
     only once the columns collapse. */
  .sip-hero-h1 {
    font-family: var(--disp);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(40px, 5.4vw, 76px);
    line-height: 1.04;
    margin: .2em 0 .26em;
    color: var(--on-accent);
  }
  /* THE SIGNATURE. A COLOUR CHANGE, and font-style is pinned to normal because
     an em is italic by user-agent default and Baloo 2 has no italic to serve —
     the browser would synthesise an oblique that fights its rounded terminals.
     :global() because Astro cannot scope markup that arrived through set:html
     (R3) — which covers both the owner's own em and the derived tail on the
     single-line path. The blush is 3.93:1 on the purple: large-text only, and
     the h1 never drops below 28px/700 in any query below. */
  .sip-hero-h1 :global(em) { font-style: normal; color: var(--pink); }
  /* Rows path only (hero.headlineLines). They FLOW as one sentence rather than
